✅ Strategies to Handle 429 Gracefully

1️⃣ Respect the Retry-After Header (if provided)
Most APIs include this header to indicate how long you should wait before retrying.

if (response.statusCode() == 429) {
    String retryAfter = response.getHeader("Retry-After");
    if (retryAfter != null) {
        Thread.sleep(Long.parseLong(retryAfter) * 1000);
    }
}


2️⃣ Implement Retry Logic with Exponential Backoff
Retry after waiting, increasing the wait time each time (1s → 2s → 4s ...)

public Response retryWithBackoff(String url, int maxRetries) {
    int retryCount = 0;
    long waitTime = 1000; // 1 second

    while (retryCount < maxRetries) {
        Response response = given().when().get(url);
        if (response.statusCode() != 429) return response;

        try {
            System.out.println("429 received. Retrying in " + waitTime + " ms...");
            Thread.sleep(waitTime);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }

        waitTime *= 2; // exponential backoff
        retryCount++;
    }

    throw new RuntimeException("Max retries exceeded due to 429 errors");
}
